  "emoji": "📱",
  "summary": "Talk about phones, apps, the internet and social media, say that something broke with اِنْكَسَرَ, and use the maṣdar اِسْتِخْدَام (using) to talk about screen time and staying safe online.",
  "intro": "Technology words in Arabic are a mix of old roots and borrowed words. Some come straight from English, like الْإِنْتَرْنِت and التِّكْنُولُوجْيَا; others are built from Arabic roots: تَطْبِيق (an app, lit. an application), مَوْقِع (a website, lit. a place) and يُحَمِّلُ (downloads, lit. loads). Two patterns you know from Week 10 help a lot today. Form VII, اِنْكَسَرَ, means that something got broken by itself: اِنْكَسَرَتْ شَاشَةُ هَاتِفِي. And the maṣdar of Form X, اِسْتِخْدَام (using), lets you make 'using the phone' the subject of a sentence: اِسْتِخْدَامُ الْإِنْتَرْنِتِ مُفِيدٌ. Listen for the rough خ in خَطَر (danger), خَاصّ (private) and اِسْتِخْدَام.",
  "objectives": [
    "Name a phone's parts and problems: the screen, the battery, charging and repairs",
